  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 325 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nCurrently, southwest flow aloft with broad ridging to our east\nand a dynamic trough/split flow to our west. Lee side surface \ntrough developed earlier from eastern Colorado northeast \nthrough the central Dakotas, in response to the western CONUS\ntrough nudging eastward and an embedded strong S/WV impulse \nrotating around its base. \n\nAs this S/WV approaches the Dakotas this aft/early eve, we will\nsee an area of precipitation developing (ongoing now over \nwestern SD north/northeast into far southwest/far south central \nND) and continuing through much of this evening, mainly along a\nsouthwest to northeast orientated boundary. Precipitation\nchances will also be supported by upper level left exit region \njet dynamics and some low/mid level frontogensis. We are still\nexpecting mostly liquid initially, with a mix or changeover to \nsome light snow once colder air filters southward into western \nND later today/this evening on the back side of the boundary and\nnear/after sunset. A few hundreths of moisture is possible \nthrough this evening, and perhaps a dusting up to a couple \ntenths of an inch of snow.\n\nNorthern stream S/WV/closed mid level low (now over southern\nALberta and Saskatchewan), swings southeast into far southern \nSK by Saturday morning, then slowly moves east along the \nInternational Border area through Sunday. This feature will \ncontribute to low chances for light snow over mainly northern \nareas of North Dakota this weekend. The main impact of the \nnorthern stream wave though will be a reinforced shot of colder \ntemperatures as an associated secondary cold front brings in \ncolder air from Canada. Our coldest period is still looking to \nbe Sunday and Monday when a large area of Canadian high pressure\ndevelops into the region, with highs still forecast in the low \n20s to low 30s both days, along with overnight lows both Sun and\nMon night in the teens (few areas in the single digits).\n\nThereafter, a progressive flow pattern is advertised by\nensembles for much of next week and through the Thanksgiving Day \nHoliday. No clear signal for any precipitation events, though \nany of the variety of weak/modest waves moving through could \nbring an occasional chance for light snow. Temperatures are also\nfavored to slowly moderate next week, with highs back in the 30s\nand 40s by Thanksgiving Day.",
